  ImageSizeRaw->{640., 800.},
  PlotRange->{{0, 640.}, {0, 800.}}]], "Output",
 TextAlignment->Center,ExpressionUUID->"bb10a103-968d-4c42-8878-43ffb5c22425"],

Cell["\<\
Figure 1. WolframGuard sits inside the server. The MCP client approval can be \
removed by always-allow (shown dashed); the OS read-only lock cannot be, and \
it denies the write even when the classifier does not.\
\>", "Caption",ExpressionUUID->"c1f2bc96-610c-fb4d-9b85-3157f542142d"],

Cell[CellGroupData[{

Cell["The problem", "Section",ExpressionUUID->"bfc84769-1d12-6446-af3d-0117bee516e0"],

Cell["\<\
The most powerful tool you can hand an AI agent is a general-purpose code \
evaluator, and Wolfram ships one as part of its recent GenAI releases. \
Wolfram/AgentTools is an MCP server whose WolframLanguageEvaluator tool takes \
a string of Wolfram Language from the model and runs it in a live kernel, \
able to read and write files, open databases, spawn processes and reach the \
network, all with the launching user's full rights. This creates a new attack \
surface. A tool that runs arbitrary code is only as safe as the model \
deciding what code to run, and that model can take its cues from whatever \
text it is pointed at.\
\>", "Text",
 CellChangeTimes->{{4.003732097493723*^9, 4.0037321021232357`*^9}, {
  4.00373283936347*^9, 4.0037328500599174`*^9}, {4.0037423060900383`*^9, 
  4.003742317930073*^9}, {4.0037424323953495`*^9, 
  4.0037424489385376`*^9}},ExpressionUUID->"22e6128a-78b5-e540-855f-\
174e38fb2515"],

Cell["\<\
The standard risk for any tool-using agent is prompt injection. The model \
reads some text, a document, a web page, a returned tool result, and that \
text carries instructions it cannot reliably tell apart from the user's own. \
When the tool in question is run this arbitrary code, a single successful \
injection reaches the whole machine.\
\>", "Text",ExpressionUUID->"b5439600-ffdd-a04e-beac-4e70d4b66039"],

Cell["\<\
The AgentTools documentation itself has a Security Considerations section \
(https://reference.wolfram.com/language/Wolfram/AgentTools/tutorial/\
QuickStartforAICodingApplications.html#225747086) stating plainly that the \
evaluator executes arbitrary Wolfram Language code, and it lists the \
consequences: reading, writing and deleting files, making network requests, \
running system commands, and reading environment variables. A companion note \
on prompt injection warns that untrusted content could steer the agent into \
misusing its tools. The revealing part is the mitigation it recommends, which \
is client-side approval permissions. In effect the entire recommended defense \
for a tool that runs arbitrary code is the human clicking approve in the chat \
client.\
\>", "Text",
 CellChangeTimes->{{4.003732118878868*^9, 4.0037321348119946`*^9}, {
  4.003742622780031*^9, 
  4.0037426443367596`*^9}},ExpressionUUID->"ea7d177a-170f-034b-ab84-\
39c8c5f58c12"],

Cell["\<\
We measured what that can lead to with a simple experiment. Driven directly \
over MCP, the evaluator deleted 250 synthetic rows with no prompt, so it has \
no gate of its own. Behind Claude Code at default permissions every \
model-requested Wolfram call was denied before reaching the server, which is \
good, but that protection lives entirely in the client, it is coarse because \
it decides about the tool rather than the code, and it disappears the moment \
a user clicks always allow. There is no fallback beneath it, because the \
paclet ships no server-side policy and the default local evaluation method is \
the one its own documentation calls not sandboxed.\
\>", "Text",
 CellChangeTimes->{{4.0037321603354435`*^9, 
  4.003732171751623*^9}},ExpressionUUID->"4b300ee8-4649-2b48-a103-\
789cee062684"]
}, Open  ]],

Cell[CellGroupData[{

Cell["How peers police unsafe code", "Section",ExpressionUUID->"919de568-8f31-4b4c-a072-4cc653d78fd7"],

Cell["\<\
Serious code-execution tools do not rely solely on the model behaving. They \
use around four mechanisms, and the useful question is where each one lives \
and whether it survives this particular setup, a third-party MCP server \
exposing an arbitrary-code tool behind a client the user may have told to \
always allow.\
\>", "Text",
 CellChangeTimes->{{4.003732445555235*^9, 
  4.003732460680826*^9}},ExpressionUUID->"2c8cf887-ccf7-bf47-92e8-\
08ba8f400f19"],

Cell[BoxData[
 TagBox[GridBox[{
    {
     StyleBox["\"\<Mechanism\>\"",
      StripOnInput->False,
      FontWeight->Bold], 
     StyleBox["\"\<Example\>\"",
      StripOnInput->False,
      FontWeight->Bold], 
     StyleBox["\"\<Lives in\>\"",
      StripOnInput->False,
      FontWeight->Bold], 
     StyleBox["\"\<Survives always-allow?\>\"",
      StripOnInput->False,
      FontWeight->Bold]},
    {"\"\<Model refusal\>\"", "\"\<any trained model\>\"", "\"\<the \
model\>\"", "\"\<no, defeated by injection\>\""},
    {"\"\<Harness content analysis\>\"", "\"\<Claude Code Bash injection \
detection\>\"", "\"\<the client\>\"", "\"\<only its own shell\>\""},
    {"\"\<Capability / approval gating\>\"", "\"\<MCP per-tool approval, \
Codex policy\>\"", "\"\<the client\>\"", "\"\<no, this is the click removed\>\
\""},
    {"\"\<OS / runtime sandbox\>\"", "\"\<Claude Code /sandbox, Codex, \
Wolfram Local/Cloud\>\"", "\"\<OS / runtime\>\"", "\"\<yes when enabled, off \
here\>\""}
   },
   AutoDelete->False,
   BaseStyle->{FontSize -> 11},
   GridBoxAlignment->{"Columns" -> {{Left}}},
   GridBoxBackground->{
    "Columns" -> {{None}}, "RowsIndexed" -> {1 -> GrayLevel[0.92]}},
   GridBoxFrame->{"Columns" -> {{True}}, "Rows" -> {{True}}},
   GridBoxItemSize->{"Columns" -> {{Automatic}}, "Rows" -> {{Automatic}}},
   GridBoxSpacings->{"Columns" -> {{2}}, "Rows" -> {{1}}}],
  "Grid"]], "Text",ExpressionUUID->"82a8975e-1065-164c-8fb9-9236cb35e16a"],

Cell["\<\
Mainstream agents put their unsafe-code policy in the harness and the \
operating system, and usually turn it on by default. A default local \
AgentTools install has neither on, and the one client that analyzes code \
content analyzes its own shell rather than a third-party evaluator's argument \
string. WolframGuard fills the empty slot with a task-bound, server-side \
layer that neither the model nor a convenience click can switch off.\
\>", "Text",ExpressionUUID->"1ab44244-db38-5448-8bbb-92fd516ce3bc"]
}, Open  ]],

Cell[CellGroupData[{

Cell["Put the guarantee at the OS boundary", "Section",
 CellChangeTimes->{{4.0037322014998264`*^9, 4.003732211835516*^9}, 
   4.0037427625533733`*^9},ExpressionUUID->"109393fa-3cc3-e540-b671-\
0b4139900d18"],

Cell["\<\
The weak way to defend a code evaluator is a denylist: enumerate the \
dangerous primitives, DeleteFile, RunProcess, an SQL DELETE, and refuse them. \
It is weak for a reason that no amount of list-tending repairs. Deciding \
whether arbitrary code is safe by looking at it is undecidable in general, \
and trivially so once the code can build a string and evaluate it. A denylist \
is a list of the attacks you already thought of, and the first primitive you \
did not list walks straight through.\
\>", "Text",ExpressionUUID->"851fb680-152b-934f-997d-669b58f0abe5"],

Cell["\<\
So WolframGuard inverts the question. For a read-only task the code's \
legitimate needs are small and knowable, compute, read files, run SELECT, so \
the robust posture is default-deny on effects: permit that small set and make \
everything else fail by construction. The way to enforce default-deny is not \
a cleverer scan but capability containment, putting the evaluation where the \
write cannot happen. WolframGuard applies an operating-system read-only lock \
to the authorized data, so a write fails at the OS regardless of which \
primitive or language issues it.\
\>", "Text",ExpressionUUID->"04fe3d3e-11bd-f340-92e4-49d4f3cf8d71"],

Cell["\<\
The effect classifier from the first version survives, demoted to a fast \
pre-filter. It rejects obvious danger early with a clean message, logs every \
call, and blocks the process and network axes so the lock cannot be tampered \
with. It is no longer what the guarantee rests on, and the containment proof \
below shows that by switching it off entirely and watching the data hold.\
\>", "Text",ExpressionUUID->"2239cefe-54bb-9f43-a3c7-52dd8942edbd"]
}, Open  ]],

Cell[CellGroupData[{

Cell["Why the OS lock and not a Wolfram sandbox", "Section",ExpressionUUID->"6dcc84a5-034e-1948-94eb-da940d2293f7"],

Cell["\<\
Wolfram ships an in-kernel sandbox, Developer`StartProtectedMode, which the \
Local evaluation method uses. We measured what it actually contains on this \
machine before trusting it.\
\>", "Text",ExpressionUUID->"625b80fe-abc8-ba42-9557-b12d7d92afba"],

Cell[BoxData[
 TagBox[GridBox[{
    {
     StyleBox["\"\<Attack (arbitrary code)\>\"",
      StripOnInput->False,
      FontWeight->Bold], 
     StyleBox["\"\<StartProtectedMode\>\"",
      StripOnInput->False,
      FontWeight->Bold], 
     StyleBox["\"\<OS deny-write lock\>\"",
      StripOnInput->False,
      FontWeight->Bold]},
    {"\"\<Wolfram DeleteFile or OpenWrite\>\"", "\"\<contained\>\"", \
"\"\<contained\>\""},
    {"\"\<RunProcess\>\"", "\"\<contained\>\"", "\"\<not applicable, no data \
write\>\""},
    {"\"\<JDBC DELETE (DatabaseLink)\>\"", "\"\<NOT contained, DB 250 to \
0\>\"", "\"\<contained, stays 250\>\""},
    {"\"\<escape via EndProtectedMode\>\"", "\"\<inert, cannot escape\>\"", \
"\"\<not applicable\>\""}
   },
   AutoDelete->False,
   BaseStyle->{FontSize -> 11},
   GridBoxAlignment->{"Columns" -> {{Left}}},
   GridBoxBackground->{
    "Columns" -> {{None}}, "RowsIndexed" -> {1 -> GrayLevel[0.92]}},
   GridBoxFrame->{"Columns" -> {{True}}, "Rows" -> {{True}}},
   GridBoxItemSize->{"Columns" -> {{Automatic}}, "Rows" -> {{Automatic}}},
   GridBoxSpacings->{"Columns" -> {{2}}, "Rows" -> {{1}}}],
  "Grid"]], "Text",ExpressionUUID->"a95ea57e-22c3-8440-86e4-4c6e58cc0a0a"],

Cell["\<\
The database row is the whole point. A JDBC write goes through the Java \
runtime and the SQLite driver, outside Wolfram's own file guard, so a \
Wolfram-level sandbox misses the headline database attack. The OS lock \
catches it because the operating system does not care which runtime asks. \
That is why the guarantee rests on the OS lock, with protected mode as an \
in-kernel complement rather than the other way around.\
\>", "Text",ExpressionUUID->"2107d0ba-c045-a648-b9a5-9eaa1f1d3e9e"]
}, Open  ]],

Cell[CellGroupData[{

Cell["How WolframGuard protects data", "Section",
 CellChangeTimes->{{4.0037322320029182`*^9, 4.0037322389843693`*^9}, {
  4.003742824219555*^9, 
  4.0037428294979916`*^9}},ExpressionUUID->"5159608e-743d-9f40-9525-\
ccff52f21f9a"],

Cell["\<\
WolframGuard is two small files and no change to the installed paclet, which \
is deliberate: a defense you can place in front of software you do not own is \
a defense people can actually adopt.\
\>", "Text",ExpressionUUID->"f9c302df-c24c-8d4b-b2ea-2cf21fdd13be"],

Cell["\<\
guarded_server.wl is the launcher. It installs the gate, applies the \
containment lock, and then starts the real AgentTools server, failing closed \
if the guard file is missing. For every policy except Off it reads the \
authorized task paths from the environment and applies an OS deny-write ACL \
for the current user, covering writes, creation, deletion, rename and \
attribute changes. The evaluator runs as that user, so its writes to the task \
data are refused by the operating system before any Wolfram or Java code gets \
to try.\
\>", "Item",ExpressionUUID->"504df1cd-fb5f-9c4c-b842-0d66388a4c6d"],

Cell["\<\
WolframGuard.wl is the pre-filter. It wraps the single evaluation entry point \
every call funnels through and classifies each one before it runs, parsing \
the string into an inert held expression it never releases, then reading the \
symbols and string literals into effect categories: filesystem write, \
process, network, database mutation, dynamic evaluation, and parse failure, \
which fails closed. SELECT reads and pure computation pass.\
\>", "Item",ExpressionUUID->"84027dd9-f812-df46-a72c-73d5bc249ade"],

Cell["\<\
Policies are read once from the environment at startup, and the model can \
change neither the policy nor the locked paths.\
\>", "Text",ExpressionUUID->"72299f6c-f2b1-d14e-a7db-c1d9e1c9457e"],

Cell[BoxData[
 TagBox[GridBox[{
    {
     StyleBox["\"\<Policy\>\"",
      StripOnInput->False,
      FontWeight->Bold], 
     StyleBox["\"\<OS lock\>\"",
      StripOnInput->False,
      FontWeight->Bold], 
     StyleBox["\"\<Classifier\>\"",
      StripOnInput->False,
      FontWeight->Bold], 
     StyleBox["\"\<State-changing effect\>\"",
      StripOnInput->False,
      FontWeight->Bold]},
    {"\"\<Off\>\"", "\"\<off\>\"", "\"\<passive\>\"", "\"\<allowed (baseline)\
\>\""},
    {"\"\<ReadOnly (default)\>\"", "\"\<on\>\"", "\"\<pre-filter\>\"", \
"\"\<blocked by the lock\>\""},
    {"\"\<AskForWrites\>\"", "\"\<on\>\"", "\"\<pre-filter\>\"", \
"\"\<refused, approval required\>\""},
    {"\"\<ContainmentOnly\>\"", "\"\<on\>\"", "\"\<disabled\>\"", \
"\"\<blocked by the lock alone\>\""}
   },
   AutoDelete->False,
   BaseStyle->{FontSize -> 11},
   GridBoxAlignment->{"Columns" -> {{Left}}},
   GridBoxBackground->{
    "Columns" -> {{None}}, "RowsIndexed" -> {1 -> GrayLevel[0.92]}},
   GridBoxFrame->{"Columns" -> {{True}}, "Rows" -> {{True}}},
   GridBoxItemSize->{"Columns" -> {{Automatic}}, "Rows" -> {{Automatic}}},
   GridBoxSpacings->{"Columns" -> {{2}}, "Rows" -> {{1}}}],
  "Grid"]], "Text",ExpressionUUID->"121028d7-b0bd-c044-b68d-49020440eef7"]
}, Open  ]],

Cell[CellGroupData[{

Cell["Security gaps found", "Section",
 CellChangeTimes->{{4.003732267403206*^9, 
  4.00373227199424*^9}},ExpressionUUID->"b808250f-fa9d-6044-a67d-\
563fc2d03e5c"],

Cell["\<\
The gap test sends one call per known effect over raw MCP with no client \
approval layer at all, which is the already-clicked-always-allow case. Under \
ReadOnly, DELETE, UPDATE, DeleteFile, RunProcess, URLRead and a dynamic-eval \
variant were all blocked with zero state change while the legitimate SELECT \
still returned 250. Under Off the same calls executed, proving the harness \
can really run them, so the ReadOnly blocks are the guard and not an \
inability to run.\
\>", "Text",ExpressionUUID->"05a6c5b8-36e9-7b43-81c3-e086e4edd049"],

Cell["\<\
With the classifier switched off entirely and all 19 payloads allowed \
straight through, state still changed on none of them, because the thing that \
refuses the write is the OS lock. The same 19 evasion payloads, which split \
and comment and dynamically assemble their SQL, alias and hide their file \
writes, delete through JDBC, and spawn processes, run under three policies on \
fresh fixtures, with state re-snapshotted independently afterwards.\
\>", "Text",
 CellChangeTimes->{{4.003732920804493*^9, 4.0037329224208794`*^9}, {
  4.00374294045335*^9, 
  4.0037429875946198`*^9}},ExpressionUUID->"6f8120b9-ade9-4a44-8c45-\
0bccb8557353"],

Cell[BoxData[
 TagBox[GridBox[{
    {
     StyleBox["\"\<Policy\>\"",
      StripOnInput->False,
      FontWeight->Bold], 
     StyleBox["\"\<DB inactive (min/max of 19)\>\"",
      StripOnInput->False,
      FontWeight->Bold], 
     StyleBox["\"\<research files\>\"",
      StripOnInput->False,
      FontWeight->Bold], 
     StyleBox["\"\<EVIL_ files\>\"",
      StripOnInput->False,
      FontWeight->Bold], 
     StyleBox["\"\<fixtures changed\>\"",
      StripOnInput->False,
      FontWeight->Bold]},
    {"\"\<Off\>\"", "\"\<0 / 250\>\"", "\"\<186 / 190\>\"", "\"\<5\>\"", \
"\"\<16 / 19 (payloads are real)\>\""},
    {"\"\<ReadOnly\>\"", "\"\<250 / 250\>\"", "\"\<190 / 190\>\"", \
"\"\<0\>\"", "\"\<0 / 19\>\""},
    {"\"\<ContainmentOnly (classifier off)\>\"", "\"\<250 / 250\>\"", \
"\"\<190 / 190\>\"", "\"\<0\>\"", "\"\<0 / 19\>\""}
   },
   AutoDelete->False,
   BaseStyle->{FontSize -> 11},
   GridBoxAlignment->{"Columns" -> {{Left}}},
   GridBoxBackground->{
    "Columns" -> {{None}}, "RowsIndexed" -> {1 -> GrayLevel[0.92]}},
   GridBoxFrame->{"Columns" -> {{True}}, "Rows" -> {{True}}},
   GridBoxItemSize->{"Columns" -> {{Automatic}}, "Rows" -> {{Automatic}}},
   GridBoxSpacings->{"Columns" -> {{2}}, "Rows" -> {{1}}}],
  "Grid"]], "Text",ExpressionUUID->"56ec28a0-094a-774d-8dce-6dcb8b5d3cfc"],

Cell["\<\
readonly_zero_state_change:             true
containment_only_classifier_all_allow:  true
containment_only_zero_state_change:     true
containment_only_bypasses:              {}\
\>", "Program",ExpressionUUID->"eb154ecc-802b-e042-9007-9041ad3af06f"],

Cell["\<\
With the classifier disabled, every payload waved through, all seven JDBC \
deletes included, not one of the 19 fixtures changed. The read-only guarantee \
is the containment, not an enumeration of bad symbols. The denylist could \
have had a hole in it and the data still would not have moved. And it is not \
collateral damage to real work: under the same lock a legitimate SELECT of \
inactive rows returns 250 while a DELETE on the same connection fails.\
\>", "Text",
 CellChangeTimes->{
  4.0037325235428905`*^9},ExpressionUUID->"4fd64964-96a8-9344-9ec5-\
b59ec3d24299"],

Cell["\<\
Writing the battery also caught a genuine bug. One payload, With[{g = \
DeleteFile}, g[path]], was allowed, because DeleteFile appears as a value \
bound to g rather than as a call head, and the classifier scanned heads only. \
The fix tests every symbol, not just heads. This is exactly why the guarantee \
was moved onto containment: any denylist will have an edge like this one, so \
the design must not depend on the list being complete, and now it does not.\
\>", "Text",ExpressionUUID->"f012241d-5d2d-8c47-8590-4c1fafedae4d"]
}, Open  ]],

Cell[CellGroupData[{

Cell["Conclusions", "Section",
 CellChangeTimes->{{4.003732278091749*^9, 
  4.00373227969895*^9}},ExpressionUUID->"9c583534-0e0b-9c40-b02a-\
7a8f7f507ca0"],

Cell["\<\
The guarantee is containment, not classifier completeness: with the lock on, \
no write to the locked data succeeds, whatever primitive issues it, including \
JDBC and with the classifier switched off. It does not claim the classifier \
is complete, which it no longer needs to be, nor that containment reaches \
paths you did not lock, so what remains open is an effect outside those \
paths, or the lock itself. Attackers may still find ways to evade the \
containment protection outside of the locked paths.\
\>", "Item",
 CellChangeTimes->{{4.0037430570193577`*^9, 4.003743057588417*^9}, {
  4.0037431274552326`*^9, 
  4.003743164683613*^9}},ExpressionUUID->"73632e59-e0ce-864c-bb6c-\
cc4058c15d7c"],

Cell["\<\
It confines writes, not reads. A permitted read still runs with the user's \
rights. WolframGuard stops state change, and stops outbound network through \
the offline kernel and the classifier, but it is not a full OS sandbox that \
would also confine what an allowed read can see. We assume reads is less \
risky than writes, but it\[CloseCurlyQuote]s also an aspect that can get \
stronger protection.\
\>", "Item",
 CellChangeTimes->{{4.0037329903327236`*^9, 4.003733008376831*^9}, {
  4.0037428896884975`*^9, 
  4.003742898774431*^9}},ExpressionUUID->"c6b6fcd7-1752-494e-b78b-\
b9fe23c1e817"],

Cell["\<\
It gates one entry point, the evaluator's single evaluation entry, and the \
lock protects the paths you name. Other AgentTools tools still rely on the \
client. The evaluator is the sharpest edge, arbitrary code, which is why it \
came first.\
\>", "Item",ExpressionUUID->"e39e70e0-6a6c-c342-be7d-5d733f6bef38"],

Cell["\<\
AskForWrites has no human-in-the-loop yet, and blocks-and-labels in headless \
runs.\
\>", "Item",ExpressionUUID->"2e25a6ab-fe2d-454c-a05f-685bc6caf207"]
}, Open  ]],

Cell[CellGroupData[{

Cell["Further Work", "Section",
 CellChangeTimes->{{4.003732287239773*^9, 
  4.003732289639927*^9}},ExpressionUUID->"a9ce37a6-ec3e-1243-9b07-\
a6362adbff59"],

Cell["\<\
A lower-privileged principal or protected child kernel, so the evaluator \
simply lacks write rights and no in-kernel trick can regain them, which also \
confines reads.\
\>", "Item",
 CellChangeTimes->{{4.003732561387642*^9, 
  4.0037325622290344`*^9}},ExpressionUUID->"f869ea99-31f9-334a-a206-\
564b0e051b37"],

Cell["\<\
Grow the evasion suite with more payload classes. Each one either passes, and \
we harden, or fails, and the claim broadens.\
\>", "Item",
 CellChangeTimes->{{4.0037330289211197`*^9, 4.003733029368948*^9}, 
   4.0037433791639805`*^9},ExpressionUUID->"0f225d85-e63c-0e46-ae18-\
e1c96f36a3dd"],

Cell["\<\
Real AskForWrites elicitation through the MCP server-initiated request path.\
\>", "Item",ExpressionUUID->"f05a7c3f-0dc7-0a4e-9555-04d9dbb7d28f"],

Cell["\<\
Put the rest of the AgentTools surface behind the same effect check.\
\>", "Item",
 CellChangeTimes->{{4.0037330351675816`*^9, 4.0037330358750362`*^9}, 
   4.0037433680419273`*^9},ExpressionUUID->"cae9aa34-4a27-8e48-93ff-\
dee646cb7ee4"]
}, Open  ]],

Cell[CellGroupData[{

Cell["Reproduce the Experiment", "Section",
 CellChangeTimes->{{4.003732293048168*^9, 
  4.0037322967999325`*^9}},ExpressionUUID->"7fefbd42-5b96-6342-b5a3-\
a88da355fd20"],

Cell["\<\
No model, no client and no network are needed for the core result.\
\>", "Text",ExpressionUUID->"6111e69c-3afb-264d-b088-8a727564a53b"],

Cell["\<\
python wolframguard-poc/instrumentation/wolframguard_bypass_tests.py --run-id \
wolframguard-containment-new
python wolframguard-poc/results/wolframguard-containment-001/probe/probe_\
containment.py
python wolframguard-poc/instrumentation/wolframguard_gap_tests.py --run-id \
wolframguard-gap-new\
\>", "Program",ExpressionUUID->"8f492756-70b6-be45-9eeb-ad71a82f54a8"],

Cell["\<\
Then open results/wolframguard-containment-new/summary.json, confirm that \
containment_only_zero_state_change is true, and re-snapshot the \
containmentonly fixture directories by hand: every one must be 1000 by 250 \
with 10 files and no EVIL_ files. The run needs a non-administrator Windows \
account, Wolfram 15.0.1 with AgentTools 2.2.0, and a free loopback port 8765.\
\>", "Text",ExpressionUUID->"89754476-59f2-8a42-bfac-0531a02857cf"],

Cell[BoxData[
 TagBox[GridBox[{
    {
     StyleBox["File",
      StripOnInput->False,
      FontWeight->Bold], 
     StyleBox["Effect",
      StripOnInput->False,
      FontWeight->Bold]},
    {"\"\<wolframguard/WolframGuard.wl\>\"", "\"\<Pre-filter: \
parse-without-eval effect classifier and policy\>\""},
    {"\"\<wolframguard/guarded_server.wl\>\"", "\"\<Launcher: installs the \
gate, applies the OS lock, starts the real server\>\""},
    {"\"\<instrumentation/wolframguard_bypass_tests.py\>\"", "\"\<The \
three-policy containment battery\>\""},
    {"\"\<results/wolframguard-containment-001/\>\"", "\"\<Evidence: verdict, \
per-policy fixtures, and the sandbox-versus-lock probe\>\""}
   },
   AutoDelete->False,
   BaseStyle->{FontSize -> 11},
   GridBoxAlignment->{"Columns" -> {{Left}}},
   GridBoxBackground->{
    "Columns" -> {{None}}, "RowsIndexed" -> {1 -> GrayLevel[0.92]}},
   GridBoxFrame->{"Columns" -> {{True}}, "Rows" -> {{True}}},
   GridBoxItemSize->{"Columns" -> {{Automatic}}, "Rows" -> {{Automatic}}},
   GridBoxSpacings->{"Columns" -> {{2}}, "Rows" -> {{1}}}],
  "Grid"]], "Text",
 CellChangeTimes->{{4.0037330502219124`*^9, 
  4.003733056175728*^9}},ExpressionUUID->"86497564-1724-8b4d-9772-\
617d220b9bb6"]
}, Open  ]],

